                    "text": "Agent: general-purpose\nDescription: Deep audit group 1\nDurationMs: 126649\nTotalTokens: 47K\n\nOutput:\nNow I have a complete picture. Here is the thorough audit:\n\n---\n\n## 1. `AlarmScheduler.kt`\n\n### 🔴 CRITICAL: Potential Infinite Loop (Lines 84–89)\n```kotlin\nwhile (ShiftSchedule.shiftFor(targetDate, brigade) != type) {\n    targetDate = targetDate.plusDays(1)\n}\n```\nIf `ShiftSchedule.shiftFor()` never returns the requested `type` for a given brigade (e.g., corrupted schedule data, or a brigade whose cycle doesn't include all shift types), this loop runs forever. Since `scheduleSingleShiftAlarm` is called from `AlarmReceiver.onReceive()` (line 79 of AlarmReceiver), this causes a **BroadcastReceiver timeout / ANR**. There is no iteration cap.\n\n### 🔴 CRITICAL: Long→Int Overflow in Request Codes (Lines 226, 259)\n```kotlin\nval requestCode = (10000 + alarmId).toInt()  // line 226\nval requestCode = (10000 + alarmId).toInt()  // line 259\n```\n`alarmId` is initialized from `System.currentTimeMillis()` (line 188 in `getRegularAlarms()`). `currentTimeMillis()` is ~1.7 × 10¹², which overflows `Int` (max ~2.1 × 10⁹). The resulting `requestCode` wraps unpredictably and **different regular alarms can collide on the same request code**, meaning `FLAG_UPDATE_CURRENT` will overwrite one alarm's PendingIntent with another's. Users will silently lose snoozed or scheduled alarms.\n\n### 🟡 Medium: Silent Alarm Scheduling Failure Goes Unnoticed (Lines 129–135)\n```kotlin\ntry {\n    if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT < Build.VERSION_CODES.S || alarmManager.canScheduleExactAlarms()) {\n        alarmManager.setAlarmClock(info, pendingIntent)\n    }\n} catch (e: Exception) {\n    Log.e(TAG, \"Ошибка установки будильника для смены\", e)\n}\n```\nIf the user has not granted `SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM` on API 31+, the `canScheduleExactAlarms()` check returns false and the alarm is **silently not scheduled**. The method returns `times.size` (line 67) as if it succeeded. The caller has no way to know the alarms were not actually set.\n\n### 🟡 Medium: Hardcoded Brigade Range (Lines 158, 318)\n```kotlin\nfor (b in 1..5) { ... }\n```\nOnly brigades 1–5 are iterated. If `cancelAllShiftAlarmsAcrossAllBrigades()` or `rescheduleAllAfterBoot()` is meant to cover all possible brigades, this is a silent data loss for brigade 6+.\n\n### 🟡 Medium: Hardcoded Cancel Limit (Line 139)\n```kotlin\nfor (index in 0 until 10) { ... }\n```\nOnly alarm indices 0–9 are cancelled. If `getAlarmTimesForShift()` ever returns >10 items, indices ≥10 will remain as stale alarms that fire but won't be re-scheduled.\n\n### 🟢 Low: `updateAutoSilenceAlarms` Is Not Daily-Recurring (Lines 270–309)\nOnly the next occurrence of each ON/OFF time is scheduled. `SilentModeReceiver` re-schedules when it fires, but if the alarm is missed (device off, killed process, etc.), auto-silence stops working permanently until the user re-configures it.\n\n### 🟢 Low: Unsafe Cast (Line 15)\n```kotlin\nval alarmManager = context.getSystemService(Context.ALARM_SERVICE) as AlarmManager\n```\nShould be `as?` for defensive coding, though this service is guaranteed to exist on all Android devices.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `AlarmReceiver.kt`\n\n### 🔴 CRITICAL: ANR from Infinite Loop in onReceive (Lines 72–89)\n```kotlin\nscheduler.scheduleSingleShiftAlarm(\n    type = shiftType,\n    brigade = brigade,\n    index = alarmIndex,\n    timeStr = alarmTime\n)\n```\nThis calls `AlarmScheduler.scheduleSingleShiftAlarm()` which contains the unbounded `while` loop (AlarmScheduler line 87). If the shift schedule doesn't contain the expected type, `onReceive` hangs → **BroadcastReceiver timeout (10s)** → ANR.\n\n### 🟡 Medium: `Int` Overflow for Notification ID (Line 34)\n```kotlin\nSystem.currentTimeMillis().toInt()\n```\nSame truncation issue. Two regular-alarm notifications fired within the same ~24-day window could get the same notification ID and overwrite each other.\n\n### 🟡 Medium: Koin Not Initialized (Line 75)\n```kotlin\nval scheduler = AlarmScheduler(context, SettingsManager(context))\n```\nThe `context` here is the receiver's restricted context. If the app process was killed and the alarm fires, the process restarts but Koin may not be fully initialized. `SettingsManager(context)` is instantiated directly (not via Koin) so it's fine, but if `SettingsManager`'s constructor depends on Koin-injected dependencies, it will crash with `MissingDefinitionException`.\n\n### 🟡 Medium: POST_NOTIFICATIONS Permission Not Checked Before notify() (Line 69)\nOn API 33+, if `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` permission is not granted, `notificationManager.notify()` is silently dropped. The user sees no alarm. The permission check is in `AlarmRingingActivity.onCreate()` (line 79–85) but that's **after** the notification is already posted — too late, and in the wrong component.\n\n---\n\n## 3. `AlarmRingingActivity.kt`\n\n### 🔴 CRITICAL: `requestDismissKeyguard` Deprecated & Insufficient (Line 68)\n```kotlin\nkeyguardManager.requestDismissKeyguard(this, null)\n```\nOn API 28+ this is deprecated. Without a `KeyguardManager.KeyguardDismissCallback`, the dismiss is fire-and-forget. If the device has a secure lock screen (PIN/pattern/biometric), the activity shows but the user **cannot interact** without unlocking first — defeating the purpose of a full-screen alarm. The `setShowWhenLocked(true)` on line 65 shows the activity over the lock screen, but swipe-to-dismiss requires touch interaction that may be blocked on secure keyguard.\n\n### 🔴 CRITICAL: Snooze PendingIntent Collision (Line 117)\n```kotlin\nval requestCode = System.currentTimeMillis().toInt()\n```\n`System.currentTimeMillis()` (~1.7 × 10¹²) truncated to Int wraps. If the user taps snooze twice within the same ~2.1-second millisecond window (unlikely but possible with fast taps), or within a window where the lower 32 bits collide, the second snooze **overwrites** the first via `FLAG_UPDATE_CURRENT`. The first snoozed alarm i